# Cloudflare Pages header injection file. Applies to every response.
#
# The two cloudflareinsights.com hosts are Cloudflare Web Analytics, enabled
# at the zone level with automatic injection: Cloudflare rewrites the HTML at
# the edge to add its beacon <script>, so the tag exists in production but not
# in dist/ or in `astro preview`. static.cloudflareinsights.com serves the
# beacon script; cloudflareinsights.com receives its POST. Without both, the
# browser blocks a script the origin itself injected, which reads as a broken
# CSP in every visitor's console. Disclosed on /privacy; asserted by
# tests/smoke/prod.spec.ts, which allows these two hosts and nothing else.
#
# The script-src and style-src hashes below cover Astro's own inline
# hydration boot scripts and its astro-island CSS reset — these are the only
# inline <script>/<style> tags this build emits (verified against dist/*.html
# for the 2026-07-16 review, P1-8). They are framework runtime output, not
# authored content, so they only change if the Astro version changes.
#
# If a future `pnpm build` fails the CSP-header e2e check (or you see
# "Refused to execute inline script/style" in the browser console), Astro's
# island runtime bytes changed — regenerate the hashes with:
#   node -e "const fs=require('fs'),c=require('crypto');const html=fs.readFileSync('dist/index.html','utf8');const re=/<(script|style)([^>]*)>([\s\S]*?)<\/\1>/g;let m;while((m=re.exec(html))){if(/\bsrc=/.test(m[2])||/application\/ld\+json/.test(m[2]))continue;console.log(m[1], 'sha256-'+c.createHash('sha256').update(m[3]).digest('base64'));}"
# and swap the two script-src hashes and the one style-src hash below. The
# JSON-LD <script type="application/ld+json"> tags do not need a hash: CSP
# script-src only governs script elements that would actually execute, and
# a non-JS type never executes as a script.
/*
  Content-Security-Policy: default-src 'self'; connect-src 'self' https://cloudflareinsights.com; script-src 'self' 'wasm-unsafe-eval' https://static.cloudflareinsights.com 'sha256-QzWFZi+FLIx23tnm9SBU4aEgx4x8DsuASP07mfqol/c=' 'sha256-SaCkFfPruIdTXT8/97JArQmGxiJAL2o4bBDvSgJ5y3Q='; style-src 'self' 'sha256-vv9IoKo7BSLbWcUHr3tNmfNVmm5L/9Cfn2H6LMk7/ow='; img-src 'self'; worker-src 'self'; object-src 'none'; base-uri 'self'; frame-ancestors 'none'; form-action 'self'
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
  Referrer-Policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin
  Permissions-Policy: camera=(), microphone=(), geolocation=()

# Everything under /_astro/ is content-addressed — the filename carries a hash
# of the bytes, so a changed file is a different URL and revalidation can never
# do anything but confirm what the browser already has. Cloudflare's default
# (max-age=14400, must-revalidate) made that round-trip mandatory, which meant
# a scan failed the moment the visitor went offline: every scan spawns a worker
# that re-imports the scanner module and the 12 MB mupdf .wasm, and a
# revalidation with no network is a failed import, not a cache hit. That broke
# the one property a visitor can check for themselves — pull the plug, scan
# anyway — so it is now immutable. Verified by tests/smoke/prod.spec.ts.
/_astro/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable
